※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4555

※ The types a generic function is called with are worked out from the types of
※ its arguments, so a type parameter standing nowhere among them is one no call
※ could settle.  Written only in what the function answers with, it would have
※ to be said at the call -- and there is no way to say it, on purpose: a call
※ that wrote its types would be a second way of saying what the arguments
※ already say everywhere else.

fn make() → T':
    0u8

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    0u6
